# This working tree is Windows with core.autocrlf=true, but these files are
# executed on Linux — the server for shell scripts, GitHub's runners for
# workflows. A stray \r there fails in ways that read as nonsense: the shebang
# picks up the carriage return, or a shell line dies with "$'\r': command not
# found". That exact error showed up twice while building the health check, so
# pin the endings rather than depending on anyone's git config.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
